and our morning rounds at home to raise  awareness of heart disease today is Go  Red For Women day that's why we're all  wearing red well the new report  estimates that nearly half of all adults  in the US have some form of heart or  blood vessel disease the increase is  mostly due to recent guideline changes  that consider high blood pressure at a  reading of 130 over 80 heart disease is  the number one killer of women but only  55 percent of them know that it's their  greatest health threat our doctor Taryn  Arul is a cardiologist at Lenox Hill  Hospital part of northwell health and a  spokesperson for the American Heart  Association welcome such an employee day  for you to be here and a perfect example  of a patient  Danil right of yours yes who on paper  looked healthy and yet there was a  mystery hidden inside her body that that  was a ticking time bomb  right and so you know this month we talk  a lot about the statistics one out of  every three women will die of  cardiovascular disease CVD kills more  women than all forms of cancer combined  but what I believe is going to change  the battle is humanizing this disease  and giving it a face the face of your  mother your sister your best friend your  co-worker in this case my patient daniil  she did not look like what a lot of  people think cardiovascular disease  looks like in a woman she was 58 she had  a little high cholesterol in a family  history that's it she didn't have high  blood pressure she didn't smoke she  wasn't overweight she wasn't diabetic  she walked 10,000 steps a day and she  had a Mediterranean diet she came to me  and she didn't have profound symptoms  she said I ate a steak dinner and I went  running to a Broadway show at a little  indigestion so I sort of blew it off I  thought it was heartburn but then it  happened again when I was running to a  doctor's appointment that feeling of a  little pressure so I'm telling you  doctor Narula  so I said okay we're gonna send you for  a test I got 